1
00:00:00,251 --> 00:00:11,939
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I found an interesting sutra from the Chinese Samyukta Āgama, number 493, and it's called Sailing Upstream.

2
00:00:15,802 --> 00:00:16,703
[SPEAKER_00]: So, thus have I heard.

3
00:00:17,583 --> 00:00:21,646
[SPEAKER_00]: On one occasion, the Venerable Sariputta addressed the monks.

4
00:00:23,638 --> 00:00:33,141
[SPEAKER_00]: If a forest monk is in an empty place in a forest or at the foot of a tree, he should undertake this training, internally observing and reflecting.

5
00:00:34,342 --> 00:00:38,703
[SPEAKER_00]: Do I perceive thoughts of sensual desire within my citta or not?

6
00:00:40,611 --> 00:00:54,563
[SPEAKER_00]: If he does not perceive them, but when encountering sense objects or a sign of beauty, craving and sensual desire arise, this opposes being withdrawn, opposes seclusion, Viveka.

7
00:00:55,544 --> 00:00:58,546
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so I think you can stop there, in a sense.

8
00:01:02,532 --> 00:01:25,483
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, so, if a forest monk goes to an empty place, like the Pali, in a forest, the foot of a tree, and, as I said before, it's like, whereas Pali, pretty much, due to the centuries of a standardization, lost all the nuances,

9
00:01:26,839 --> 00:01:32,781
[SPEAKER_01]: The Pali would just say, you know, a man goes to a forest, foot of a tree, empty hut, purifies his mind from unwholesome states.

10
00:01:33,641 --> 00:01:34,181
[SPEAKER_00]: Right.

11
00:01:34,481 --> 00:01:43,664
[SPEAKER_01]: And today, purification, in the contemporary sense, purification of unwholesome states, pretty much, it's open to any interpretation whatsoever.

12
00:01:43,884 --> 00:01:53,067
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whatever you would have, say, acquired in a course of time, whatever ideas you would have acquired that the Buddhist practice is all about,

13
00:01:53,885 --> 00:01:58,369
[SPEAKER_01]: You would interpret purification of thoughts according to those ideas.

14
00:01:58,549 --> 00:02:08,037
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you think purification of thoughts comes down to diligently sitting and watching your sensations, you would... that's how you would read the sutta.

15
00:02:08,397 --> 00:02:11,980
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is no nuances in the Pali.

16
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:20,747
[SPEAKER_01]: Especially, like, when it comes to this particular topic of, you know, the citta and sign of beauty and stuff, it's sort of...

17
00:02:22,567 --> 00:02:41,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, like, fragmented and just template phrases, that, as I said, it's like without, like, having not discovered, say, these āgamas, or these extra kind of inputs from āgamas, you would be sort of just, well, a lot would be left to, like, a wide range of interpretations.

18
00:02:41,220 --> 00:02:41,701
[SPEAKER_01]: Right, right.

19
00:02:42,041 --> 00:02:47,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Whereby pretty much anything can fit into, say, the idea of purification of thoughts.

20
00:02:47,545 --> 00:02:48,986
[SPEAKER_01]: Any, any practice can fit into it.

21
00:02:49,026 --> 00:02:51,388
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, I'm purifying thoughts by doing this, this or that.

22
00:02:52,125 --> 00:02:58,489
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, again, there is nothing in between that's explained that you could see that actually no, like these things wouldn't fit, or these things would fit more, and so on.

23
00:02:59,350 --> 00:03:15,080
[SPEAKER_01]: And, as I said, what's even more interesting is that all these āgamas, as it says even at the end of this one, were translated from the Pali.

24
00:03:15,821 --> 00:03:17,242
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they were Pali translations.

25
00:03:18,662 --> 00:03:26,950
