26
00:03:27,870 --> 00:03:31,173
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means Pali, back then, was saying this.

27
00:03:31,794 --> 00:03:31,974
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

28
00:03:32,555 --> 00:03:35,217
[SPEAKER_01]: It wasn't like this was written on its own.

29
00:03:35,277 --> 00:03:42,523
[SPEAKER_01]: This is translated from Pali when those missionaries came down to, like, Sri Lanka and started translating the existing text.

30
00:03:42,804 --> 00:03:44,645
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, in the course of time,

31
00:03:45,652 --> 00:03:52,018
[SPEAKER_01]: It was the Pali that got changed, that got even more standardized, and lost the nuances.

32
00:03:52,899 --> 00:03:56,082
[SPEAKER_01]: They were already forms of standardization.

33
00:03:58,084 --> 00:03:58,725
[SPEAKER_00]: See what I mean?

34
00:03:58,745 --> 00:04:00,647
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, fourth, fifth century,

35
00:04:01,563 --> 00:04:07,287
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already like, what, six... well, ten centuries after the Buddha died.

36
00:04:07,307 --> 00:04:08,548
[SPEAKER_01]: It's already standardized.

37
00:04:08,628 --> 00:04:11,651
[SPEAKER_01]: But now you can see how much even more standardized, i.e.

38
00:04:12,111 --> 00:04:15,774
[SPEAKER_01]: watered down, turned into template, losing the nuances, is today.

39
00:04:16,054 --> 00:04:29,564
[SPEAKER_01]: So, discovering the Agamas, there were translations of some period in between, shows you that actually Pali has undergone quite a significant reduction in the amount of nuances it contains.

40
00:04:32,075 --> 00:04:33,376
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like...

41
00:04:33,416 --> 00:04:35,658
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it definitely adds to reading the suttas.

42
00:04:35,818 --> 00:04:36,259
[SPEAKER_01]: For sure.

43
00:04:36,359 --> 00:04:56,655
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said, like, this is, you know, how many people would have read the, you know, a forest monk goes to an empty place, a root of a tree, and purifies his mind of unwholesome states, purifies his mind of the five hindrances, and, like, not really looked into it further, because there's nothing else to look into.

44
00:04:56,855 --> 00:04:58,317
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you would naturally

45
00:04:58,837 --> 00:05:08,899
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to resort to whatever other kind of contemporary interpretations are provided.

46
00:05:09,039 --> 00:05:16,781
[SPEAKER_01]: And usually, that kind of comes through commentaries and the mainstream views, which is like, yes, purification of hindrances means meditation technique.

47
00:05:17,261 --> 00:05:18,642
[SPEAKER_01]: There is nothing else that you can see.

48
00:05:19,082 --> 00:05:24,023
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you could, if you, like, start studying the whole body of Pali, you would see that it doesn't add up.

49
00:05:24,103 --> 00:05:25,223
[SPEAKER_01]: But most people don't do that.

50
00:05:25,303 --> 00:05:26,864
[SPEAKER_01]: Most people arrive to one sutta and then

51
00:05:27,644 --> 00:05:35,532
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you wouldn't necessarily be questioning that, even if you come across the suttas, eventually, that are contradicting those very views, that are not adding up.

52
00:05:35,652 --> 00:05:37,513
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in this specific instance, it's like

53
00:05:53,923 --> 00:06:00,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, how many times have you read Forest Monk's Ghost, Purifies His Mind, Freezes His Mind from Hindrances, from Unwholesome States?

54
00:06:00,948 --> 00:06:11,716
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, just as I said, because it's so generic, so condensed, so template-like, any practice you were presented with, as a Buddhist practice,

55
00:06:13,622 --> 00:06:18,326
[SPEAKER_01]: All I need to do is tell you that's what purification of mind means.

56
00:06:18,687 --> 00:06:19,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Counting your breath.

57
00:06:20,108 --> 00:06:21,229
[SPEAKER_01]: Watching sensations.

58
00:06:21,709 --> 00:06:22,890
[SPEAKER_01]: Feeling the soles of your feet.

59
00:06:22,950 --> 00:06:23,491
[SPEAKER_01]: Mantra.

60
00:06:23,531 --> 00:06:24,272
[SPEAKER_01]: Repeat Buddha.

61
00:06:24,912 --> 00:06:32,419
[SPEAKER_01]: And you would have, like, an average person would have no reference as to, like, does this add up?

62
00:06:32,659 --> 00:06:33,520
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there more to this?

63
00:06:33,560 --> 00:06:36,583
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's just, okay, that's what it's connected in.

64
00:06:37,743 --> 00:06:39,824
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, nothing says that it's wrong, so to speak.

65
00:06:40,224 --> 00:06:43,304
[SPEAKER_01]: Unless, as I said, you start studying the entire.

66
00:06:43,344 --> 00:06:49,105
[SPEAKER_01]: Which most people won't do or, you know, will kind of stop halfway through, so to speak.

67
00:06:49,606 --> 00:06:56,067
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, even if they do, will not necessarily turn them into questioning the preconceived notions of interpretations that they already started with.

68
00:06:56,107 --> 00:06:58,227
[SPEAKER_01]: So, this is obviously a welcome input.

69
00:06:58,547 --> 00:07:05,329
[SPEAKER_01]: Any form of, like, āgama, in this sense, it's like a direct translation of what Pali used to say.

70
00:07:06,843 --> 00:07:15,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you can't find the exactly matching sutta, you could find the passages that correspond and this adds to it.

71
00:07:15,848 --> 00:07:18,469
[SPEAKER_01]: And in this specific instance, it's like, actually says...

72
00:07:18,990 --> 00:07:21,471
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't think there's any Pali that says this so clearly.

73
00:07:22,051 --> 00:07:36,519
[SPEAKER_01]: That when a monk goes into seclusion, goes into his forest, root of a tree, he reflects thus, saying, do I perceive thoughts of sensual desire within my citta or not?

74
00:07:39,155 --> 00:07:46,602
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, internally observing, internal observations and thinking.

75
00:07:47,363 --> 00:07:58,894
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, as I said, because it's already a monk, means you're already, say, purified in your external behavior, you're keeping the precepts and so on, and then it's like you go into a solitary dwelling,

76
00:08:03,053 --> 00:08:09,535
[SPEAKER_01]: And begin, pretty much, by the recognition of me and the animal, the citta.

77
00:08:09,595 --> 00:08:17,278
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the citta would have already been seen sufficiently through purification of physical behavior, as we explained so many other times.

78
00:08:17,318 --> 00:08:18,599
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like...

79
00:08:18,639 --> 00:08:26,022
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is no more confusion as to where to look, that I'm internally observing.

80
00:08:26,682 --> 00:08:29,623
[SPEAKER_01]: You know when you're internally observing, you're looking at the state of the animal.

81
00:08:30,503 --> 00:08:32,464
[SPEAKER_01]: The overall state of the mind, of the citta.

82
00:08:32,925 --> 00:08:36,867
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll stop saying mind, just citta, which is obviously the mind, but...

83
00:08:36,887 --> 00:08:41,630
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's like, are there thoughts of sensuality within this citta?

84
00:08:42,070 --> 00:08:59,722
[SPEAKER_01]: So, is this animal, here and now, as I'm sitting, withdrawn, physically withdrawn, sitting in a quiet, secluded environment, are there thoughts that are connected, concerned, in regard to, with sensuality, within this citta?

85
00:09:00,982 --> 00:09:04,384
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, not so that I can do this technique, so that I can apply that.

86
00:09:04,605 --> 00:09:04,845
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

87
00:09:04,945 --> 00:09:12,210
[SPEAKER_01]: Just a simple reflection and recognition of the internal domain, right now, as I'm sitting here alone with Drone.

88
00:09:13,691 --> 00:09:23,438
[SPEAKER_01]: Just ask that question in the neutrality of that reflection, not for the sake of judgment, not for the sake of conclusion, just

89
00:09:24,336 --> 00:09:28,937
[SPEAKER_01]: Are there thoughts, right now, in this citta, that are concerned with sensuality?

90
00:09:29,617 --> 00:09:33,778
[SPEAKER_01]: Sexuality, sensuality, any, any kind of form of it.

91
00:09:34,298 --> 00:09:40,500
[SPEAKER_01]: And look for the answer in the state of the citta, not in the state of like, oh, this, I must be like that.

92
00:09:40,520 --> 00:09:41,260
[SPEAKER_00]: But yeah, that's the thing.

93
00:09:41,280 --> 00:09:51,222
[SPEAKER_00]: So when, you know, I'll be sitting and doing that same thing and thinking, okay, are there any sensual thoughts in the citta?

94
00:09:52,163 --> 00:09:53,543
[SPEAKER_00]: And so you can see, oh yeah,

95
00:09:54,323 --> 00:09:56,504
[SPEAKER_00]: The citta is inclined...

96
00:09:56,544 --> 00:10:01,727
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not thinking sensual thoughts, but it is inclined towards sensuality.

97
00:10:02,227 --> 00:10:03,888
[SPEAKER_00]: It wants to go in a certain direction.

98
00:10:03,908 --> 00:10:04,749
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's the next one.

99
00:10:05,169 --> 00:10:05,549
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yes.

100
00:10:05,609 --> 00:10:08,491
[SPEAKER_01]: First, it's like, are the thoughts of sensuality?

101
00:10:09,211 --> 00:10:10,752
[SPEAKER_01]: If he does not perceive them,

102
00:10:11,837 --> 00:10:18,999
[SPEAKER_01]: But when encountering sense objects or a sign of beauty or a thought, craving and sensual desire arise, this opposes to being withdrawn.

103
00:10:19,039 --> 00:10:23,340
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the answer to the first question might be like, oh no, I'm not.

104
00:10:23,420 --> 00:10:25,681
[SPEAKER_01]: There are no thoughts currently besetting the mind.

105
00:10:26,481 --> 00:10:28,522
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, that's a neutral recognition.

106
00:10:29,342 --> 00:10:32,883
[SPEAKER_01]: And that recognition is informed by the state of citta.

107
00:10:34,298 --> 00:10:40,840
[SPEAKER_01]: Did not come from you as you answering what you think the answer should be.

108
00:10:41,620 --> 00:10:42,380
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the difference.

109
00:10:43,060 --> 00:10:51,082
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, that should be the answer, but you're not looking for it to come from that place, but you want it to come from the recognition of citta, which means you're open to any answer.

110
00:10:52,562 --> 00:10:54,563
[SPEAKER_01]: So, are there thoughts of sensuality connected with citta?

111
00:10:56,548 --> 00:10:56,708
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

112
00:10:57,289 --> 00:10:58,490
[SPEAKER_01]: At least right now, no.

113
00:10:59,171 --> 00:11:00,212
[SPEAKER_01]: The next one is okay.

114
00:11:00,652 --> 00:11:05,457
[SPEAKER_01]: If, however, I were to think about the sign of beauty.

115
00:11:05,697 --> 00:11:06,318
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what it says.

116
00:11:07,139 --> 00:11:10,522
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the same way when he reflects on the perception of beauty.

117
00:11:10,762 --> 00:11:16,208
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm not going and physically exposing my senses to unsuitable sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches.

118
00:11:17,305 --> 00:11:35,811
[SPEAKER_01]: That's been dealt with through withdrawal, through taming on that level, but now it's like, okay, if I were to bring up a sign of beauty, a sign of attractive, a sign of that which is incentive, inciting of lust, as the Pāli Sutta say, would then the citta turn towards it?

119
00:11:36,351 --> 00:11:40,432
[SPEAKER_01]: Would then the citta leap with excitement towards it?

120
00:11:41,412 --> 00:11:43,193
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you could already know the answer.

121
00:11:45,092 --> 00:11:55,529
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, okay, so I'll bring up a thought again, while keeping a corner of my eye on the animal, that feeling, on that mood, on that generality of, like, the state that I feel.

122
00:11:56,491 --> 00:11:57,673
[SPEAKER_01]: Will there be a ripple in it?

123
00:11:57,713 --> 00:11:58,694
[SPEAKER_01]: Will there be a movement?

124
00:11:58,955 --> 00:11:59,395
[SPEAKER_01]: If so,

125
00:12:00,512 --> 00:12:02,353
[SPEAKER_01]: citta is not free from sensuality.

126
00:12:02,553 --> 00:12:05,934
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, not so that I can resolve it here and now, this very second.

127
00:12:06,054 --> 00:12:10,055
[SPEAKER_01]: More like just to see the level of response that comes from the animal.

128
00:12:10,555 --> 00:12:13,416
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's literally like... like you have a dog tied down.

129
00:12:13,737 --> 00:12:14,297
[SPEAKER_01]: Calm down.

130
00:12:14,497 --> 00:12:15,797
[SPEAKER_01]: He's sitting next to you on a leash.

131
00:12:17,598 --> 00:12:29,202
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, you don't know if the dog is calm because recently there hasn't been anything that's been provoking it, or because it has actually internally reached the state of, oh, this is a trained dog,

132
00:12:30,252 --> 00:12:31,573
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's waiting for your instruction.

133
00:12:31,913 --> 00:12:32,273
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

134
00:12:32,373 --> 00:12:33,373
[SPEAKER_01]: It won't act on its own.

135
00:12:33,474 --> 00:12:33,814
[SPEAKER_01]: It's on.

136
00:12:34,334 --> 00:12:40,197
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to, as I said, expose the triggers to see if the dog will twitch.

137
00:12:41,297 --> 00:12:50,681
[SPEAKER_01]: And in this sense, those triggers are not going back to sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches of unsuitable kind, but the triggers are, as the actual āgama says, reflecting on the perception of beauty.

138
00:12:51,682 --> 00:12:54,483
[SPEAKER_01]: So, having the thoughts in a controlled environment.

139
00:12:55,924 --> 00:12:56,124
[SPEAKER_01]: So,

140
00:12:58,412 --> 00:13:04,074
[SPEAKER_01]: If the dog reacts because it's a controlled environment, it won't go too far.

141
00:13:04,254 --> 00:13:09,357
[SPEAKER_01]: It will have given you a sufficient, suitable... That's enough of a feedback.

142
00:13:09,397 --> 00:13:12,298
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't need it to go really wild, running after stuff.

143
00:13:12,378 --> 00:13:15,019
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's exactly what the paragraph in the Sutta says.

144
00:13:15,719 --> 00:13:24,423
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if he doesn't perceive the current thoughts, but when the citta, when the animal encounters the sense object or sign of beauty,

145
00:13:26,487 --> 00:13:34,493
[SPEAKER_01]: Craving essential desire arises and that it's just like a man forcefully rowing a boat upstream against the current.

146
00:13:34,933 --> 00:13:36,755
[SPEAKER_01]: So that is your current state then.

147
00:13:37,455 --> 00:13:51,727
[SPEAKER_01]: If my citta is calm, because I'm keeping it calm, I'm keeping it withdrawn, I'm not exposing it to unsuitable sides, which is what you should do, but it does react if things were to come within its range, it's like me rowing a boat upstream.

148
00:13:52,570 --> 00:13:56,471
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means it's going in the right direction for as long as I'm rowing.

149
00:13:56,511 --> 00:13:56,691
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

150
00:13:57,031 --> 00:14:00,993
[SPEAKER_01]: But if I stop rowing, the boat goes back downstream immediately.

151
00:14:01,973 --> 00:14:03,233
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not enough.

152
00:14:03,453 --> 00:14:05,374
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not sufficient.

153
00:14:06,414 --> 00:14:15,197
[SPEAKER_01]: And certainly, as the sutta says, like, he wouldn't be... a monk who recognizes that would not dare to declare of himself

154
00:14:17,331 --> 00:14:27,044
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, my mind is liberated through dispassion regarding the five codes of sensual desire, because it knows that if circumstances were to change, if I were to be exposed to unsuitable sight,

155
00:14:28,097 --> 00:14:34,463
[SPEAKER_01]: My mind, basically, unless I'm sustaining the effort of rowing up the stream, my mind will go back.

156
00:14:34,663 --> 00:14:35,864
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's not good enough.

157
00:14:36,125 --> 00:14:48,536
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's the thing that people can... a monk can go into the forest and so on, sit down and and not be exposed, even avoid sensual thoughts and so on, and be, you know, secluded in that sense.

158
00:14:50,438 --> 00:14:54,459
[SPEAKER_00]: But, he doesn't, he doesn't bring these little triggers up, does he?

159
00:14:54,559 --> 00:15:00,261
[SPEAKER_00]: He doesn't, he doesn't think on the possibility of, oh, I could go towards your sensuality in there.

160
00:15:00,281 --> 00:15:14,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, see, that's, like, you, you find, now, when you, when we sort of expound on this founding āgama, you get to see, like, oh, yeah, there are suttas in Pali that say things such as

161
00:15:14,477 --> 00:15:19,501
[SPEAKER_01]: Can unreasonable, sensual desire arise in me, if I were to attend to things wrongly?

162
00:15:20,501 --> 00:15:30,829
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there is that training through the triggers and stuff, but without the additional context, such as, like, which this Agama provides, for example.

163
00:15:31,869 --> 00:15:39,755
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, it can mean anything, but now you have slightly different input, and now you get to recognize other things in Pali that actually talk about the same.

164
00:15:41,365 --> 00:15:42,326
[SPEAKER_01]: And that would be exactly that.

165
00:15:42,366 --> 00:15:44,688
[SPEAKER_01]: So, okay, there is no current desire.

166
00:15:45,529 --> 00:15:46,609
[SPEAKER_01]: That is your work, to reckon.

167
00:15:46,630 --> 00:15:47,590
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there a current desire?

168
00:15:47,750 --> 00:15:49,872
[SPEAKER_01]: Are there currently thoughts besetting the citta?

169
00:15:50,513 --> 00:15:50,673
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

170
00:15:51,574 --> 00:15:54,796
[SPEAKER_01]: Could there be desire if the thoughts were to change?

171
00:15:55,817 --> 00:16:03,644
[SPEAKER_01]: And you, as I said, even without going into the specifics of the thoughts, you could already see if there is a trigger, if the dog twitches, if like, oh, is there something?

172
00:16:04,545 --> 00:16:05,646
