00:31:51,486 --> 00:31:54,327
[SPEAKER_00]: The chances of now that animal being tamed...

343
00:31:55,328 --> 00:31:57,309
[SPEAKER_00]: Wow, you're just throwing commands... No.

344
00:31:57,369 --> 00:32:01,452
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the only person that doesn't understand the principle of training would be trying that.

345
00:32:01,632 --> 00:32:16,380
[SPEAKER_01]: And you see that again, like, you see that when somebody... when you show somebody how to get their own dog to sit, and then they think you speak... you're a whisperer, you speak a magic language that dogs understand, because they can't read, you know.

346
00:32:16,740 --> 00:32:19,382
[SPEAKER_01]: You say it when there's an eye contact with the dog.

347
00:32:20,382 --> 00:32:24,685
[SPEAKER_01]: When you get its attention, you know, now if I say the command, it will respond.

348
00:32:26,211 --> 00:32:29,434
[SPEAKER_01]: But, without getting its eye contact, that's, like, one of the basic things.

349
00:32:29,514 --> 00:32:40,162
[SPEAKER_01]: Without getting the eye contact first, eye... attention is on me, you would see this person, then, trying to emulate what they saw you do, and just start saying, sit, sit, sit, sit.

350
00:32:40,182 --> 00:32:40,382
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

351
00:32:40,462 --> 00:32:43,325
[SPEAKER_01]: And dog is, like, from the start, hasn't paid attention to them.

352
00:32:43,385 --> 00:32:44,506
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, it's not sitting.

353
00:32:44,526 --> 00:32:44,726
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

354
00:32:45,206 --> 00:32:49,950
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, you get him to pay attention to you, even for a millisecond, and in that millisecond, you tell it to sit, and it sits.

355
00:32:51,637 --> 00:32:55,680
[SPEAKER_01]: And externally, for somebody who doesn't understand how training works, it looks like magic.

356
00:32:56,120 --> 00:33:03,804
[SPEAKER_00]: So, yeah, so you need to find the animal and the animal needs to see, find you.

357
00:33:04,125 --> 00:33:06,566
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why, like, it's not negotiable, the whole...

358
00:33:08,508 --> 00:33:10,230
[SPEAKER_01]: The virtue, the precepts...

359
00:33:10,310 --> 00:33:24,750
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you can't do the eight precepts, your training then, of that striving saṅkhara, or rowing your boat up the stream, has to be concerned with, okay, increasing my capacity to be able to keep the eight precepts.

360
00:33:26,033 --> 00:33:31,779
[SPEAKER_01]: To be celibate, to be withdrawn from sensuality, in those, as I said, outwardly coarse manners.

361
00:33:32,400 --> 00:33:39,407
[SPEAKER_01]: If I'm failing that, there is no subtle recognition of the state of my German shepherd that I'm training.

362
00:33:39,427 --> 00:33:47,375
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it's just... Conditionally, I can maybe containing every now and then, but generally I'm very much under its own rule, not other way around.

363
00:33:50,480 --> 00:33:51,642
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a danger there.

364
00:33:52,063 --> 00:34:04,963
[SPEAKER_00]: You think you're practicing, but if you're not seeing this feedback, that your mind is not turning away from these possibilities of sensuality, then, the thing is, your striving is going to end sometime.

365
00:34:05,383 --> 00:34:05,564
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

366
00:34:05,884 --> 00:34:10,285
[SPEAKER_00]: You're gonna get sick and your little circumstantial spiritual life...

367
00:34:11,266 --> 00:34:18,088
[SPEAKER_01]: Sooner or later, whatever circumstantially you would have built up, if it stays circumstantial, will have to go away.

368
00:34:18,148 --> 00:34:19,788
[SPEAKER_00]: And the mind's gonna go back to what it's...

369
00:34:19,808 --> 00:34:21,449
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, which means then...

370
00:34:21,489 --> 00:34:23,549
[SPEAKER_00]: Flows forcefully in the direction it wants to go.

371
00:34:24,690 --> 00:34:32,012
[SPEAKER_01]: Because people, by not recognizing that it's a big danger, that's how people remain very much within the scope of that danger.

372
00:34:32,152 --> 00:34:33,452
[SPEAKER_01]: By thinking, oh, it's not a problem.

373
00:34:33,692 --> 00:34:35,233
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, because I feel great now.

374
00:34:35,473 --> 00:34:35,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

375
00:34:37,710 --> 00:34:55,017
[SPEAKER_01]: And it it's not a problem feeling great now, but you need to recognize that or reflect on whether you are feeling great now, whether that comes on account of you have circumstantially protected yourself through the right environment and this and that and having nothing trigger you.

376
00:34:56,040 --> 00:35:04,122
[SPEAKER_01]: Or having every ready psychological kind of rationalizations ready, whenever triggers come up, to like quickly...

377
00:35:04,142 --> 00:35:10,164
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, but that's that, that's this, and dismiss it, and not have the the state of the agitated animal that you have to endure.

378
00:35:10,764 --> 00:35:14,004
[SPEAKER_01]: Or you recognize that your peace comes from all of not doing any of that.

379
00:35:14,305 --> 00:35:23,527
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's like, I've done this right circumstances, provided and stuff, but then I've also endured the agitation of the animal without acting out rightly, learned to read its signs directly,

380
00:35:24,549 --> 00:35:30,331
[SPEAKER_01]: Having not acted out in any ways, brought up the triggers, so to see whether the animal will stay calm.

381
00:35:30,451 --> 00:35:35,033
[SPEAKER_01]: If it doesn't stay calm, I repeat, you know, the broadening of the context, the danger and stuff.

382
00:35:35,513 --> 00:35:37,093
[SPEAKER_01]: And now it's calm, and now it's acting.

383
00:35:37,113 --> 00:35:39,174
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, oh, now I can actually relax.

384
00:35:39,254 --> 00:35:41,555
[SPEAKER_01]: Like the two types of thoughts that it has.

385
00:35:42,115 --> 00:35:47,257
[SPEAKER_01]: After a while, he would, you know, the shepherd can go to sleep, basically, and the cows would still be there.

386
00:35:47,297 --> 00:35:52,739
[SPEAKER_01]: They would not wander back into the unsuitable pastures like how they used to do.

387
00:35:53,982 --> 00:36:03,949
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's like, okay, that piece is fine, and you can make your claim and your attainments and stuff, but if it's circumstantial, or if you're not sure, means it's circumstantial, basically.

388
00:36:04,749 --> 00:36:19,680
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, when conditions change, you end up in a hospital or something, where you cannot micromanage the input that comes through your senses and stuff, or resort to your meditation technique in a perfect silence, in an artificial environment of a meditation retreat,

389
00:36:20,806 --> 00:36:22,187
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like... You can't manage.

390
00:36:22,467 --> 00:36:25,028
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, the animal would just be as wild as it always was.

391
00:36:25,588 --> 00:36:29,429
[SPEAKER_00]: You want the thing to be free and to be doing the right things by itself.

392
00:36:29,729 --> 00:36:30,169
[SPEAKER_01]: By itself.

393
00:36:30,249 --> 00:36:30,790
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the goal.

394
00:36:31,850 --> 00:36:33,631
[SPEAKER_00]: You want it to be liberated from...

395
00:36:33,911 --> 00:36:34,251
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

396
00:36:34,331 --> 00:36:36,032
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why he knows it's liberated.

397
00:36:38,546 --> 00:37:01,483
[SPEAKER_00]: Notice that, you know, when a... that which used to... or that which makes you angry, you get angry from, and then you encounter things that are invocative of anger, provocative, sorry, of anger, and the mind... you don't have to tell the mind anything, and it just...

398
00:37:02,583 --> 00:37:05,064
[SPEAKER_00]: It turns away from that, like, experience.

399
00:37:05,144 --> 00:37:07,965
[SPEAKER_01]: It might even surprise you, in that sense.

400
00:37:08,265 --> 00:37:24,450
[SPEAKER_01]: If you've been training right, you don't necessarily, because obviously you're not seeking out the core triggers for sensuality or your will, operating on the level of thoughts, apprehensions of the sign of beauty or the ugly, of the provocative things, one way or the other.

401
00:37:25,630 --> 00:37:26,190
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're doing.

402
00:37:26,230 --> 00:37:26,871
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your work.

403
00:37:26,951 --> 00:37:27,791
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your concern.

404
00:37:28,031 --> 00:37:36,135
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, something does enter through your eyes, through your nose, through some... that used to... and then you... you kind of... you're braced.

405
00:37:36,336 --> 00:37:41,138
[SPEAKER_01]: You're waiting for the raging animal, because this is my trigger for anger.

406
00:37:42,199 --> 00:37:42,799
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you look.

407
00:37:42,979 --> 00:37:44,940
[SPEAKER_01]: The animal is actually laying down.

408
00:37:45,020 --> 00:37:46,941
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you realize, oh, it's calm.

409
00:37:47,221 --> 00:37:48,142
[SPEAKER_01]: The training works.

410
00:37:49,208 --> 00:37:50,429
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't do this.

411
00:37:50,489 --> 00:37:51,270
[SPEAKER_01]: It chose this.

412
00:37:51,730 --> 00:37:53,752
[SPEAKER_01]: I was actually ready for it to start barking.

413
00:37:54,292 --> 00:37:54,652
[SPEAKER_01]: It didn't.

414
00:37:55,053 --> 00:38:02,979
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why those examples in the suttas... monks suddenly faced a prospect of death.

415
00:38:03,760 --> 00:38:06,102
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when they realized that the work was done.

416
00:38:06,322 --> 00:38:07,042
[SPEAKER_01]: Not before that.

417
00:38:07,423 --> 00:38:08,464
[SPEAKER_01]: The work was actually done.

418
00:38:08,624 --> 00:38:09,785
[SPEAKER_01]: They were arahants before that.

419
00:38:10,548 --> 00:38:22,178
[SPEAKER_01]: But they just were not necessarily certain about it, because nothing provoked the mind to the extent of them to see that, oh, even death, the mind is calm, didn't move.

420
00:38:22,599 --> 00:38:23,780
[SPEAKER_01]: Therefore, it's liberated.

421
00:38:23,840 --> 00:38:27,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Therefore, I know now that it's liberated, because this has proven it to me.

422
00:38:28,764 --> 00:38:33,969
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when they, when that monk almost, I don't know, stumble upon these people about to set somebody on fire, and then they want to kill him,

423
00:38:35,780 --> 00:38:38,161
[SPEAKER_01]: And his mind became liberated there and then.

424
00:38:38,241 --> 00:38:39,601
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you think, oh, he was so lucky.

425
00:38:39,641 --> 00:38:40,322
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, well, no.

426
00:38:40,762 --> 00:38:41,722
[SPEAKER_01]: He ordered it, did the work.

427
00:38:42,282 --> 00:38:58,328
[SPEAKER_01]: There and then, he recognized for himself that the animal was calm, because the extreme scenario that he could not have even imagined in its kind of extremeness, he was ready for the mind to start kicking and screaming, defending itself, or being affected by fear.

428
00:38:59,638 --> 00:39:02,320
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, he looked back and the mind was completely unmoved.

429
00:39:03,080 --> 00:39:08,463
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it came as a surprise to him, but not as a shock, because he knows why that was.

430
00:39:08,543 --> 00:39:12,085
[SPEAKER_01]: He just wasn't... didn't expect it, that it's going to be there and then, in that instance.

431
00:39:13,046 --> 00:39:15,167
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, like, the path of Theraterigaptha.

432
00:39:15,187 --> 00:39:15,868
[SPEAKER_01]: It's all about...

433
00:39:16,867 --> 00:39:40,161
[SPEAKER_01]: The sudden recognitions of the mind, mind being liberated or calm, after, like, I don't know, doing relentless, for who knows how long, reflection on the right things, like, like bleeding out of the feet, because he was just reflecting on stuff so long, that he was walking back and forth, forgot for how long, and his feet started to split and bleed.

434
00:39:40,181 --> 00:39:45,565
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's like, okay, I'll stop this, too much for now, you know, wash my feet, and as the mud came off the feet,

435
00:39:46,245 --> 00:39:47,886
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize, oh, the mind was calm.

436
00:39:48,327 --> 00:39:52,630
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because I was washing the feet in my river, but because all the prior work of taming.

437
00:39:53,631 --> 00:39:58,074
[SPEAKER_01]: I was so concerned with providing the right context, maintaining the right danger.

438
00:39:58,274 --> 00:40:01,277
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't notice that the dog is actually not moving anyway.

439
00:40:01,317 --> 00:40:01,777
[SPEAKER_01]: He stopped.

440
00:40:01,837 --> 00:40:02,578
[SPEAKER_01]: He was already calm.

441
00:40:02,598 --> 00:40:03,599
[SPEAKER_00]: No more training needed.

442
00:40:03,799 --> 00:40:05,020
[SPEAKER_01]: No more training needed, yeah.

443
00:40:07,462 --> 00:40:11,985
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I think it was the monk who was walking up and down, then he got up to walk, and then he fell over.

444
00:40:12,045 --> 00:40:14,286
[SPEAKER_01]: Fell, he couldn't, yeah, he was just so tired, he just couldn't walk anymore.

445
00:40:14,306 --> 00:40:15,847
[SPEAKER_00]: But then, at that point, he realized, oh...

446
00:40:16,087 --> 00:40:25,933
[SPEAKER_01]: But see, again, like, you read just that, without the additional context, well, an uninstructed person, not familiar with the early suttas,

447
00:40:26,983 --> 00:40:40,187
[SPEAKER_01]: What one would, or quite often, ends up concluding is, okay, so if I just do relentlessly determined walking up and down, so the act of walking is what purified me.

448
00:40:40,367 --> 00:40:40,587
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

449
00:40:40,907 --> 00:40:49,790
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like, no, it's the act of purifying your mind according to these standards, according to these descriptions, while walking.

450
00:40:52,160 --> 00:40:57,843
[SPEAKER_01]: But, yeah, if you just read what the little bit of condensed Pali says, oh, yeah, you just walk back and forth.

451
00:40:57,983 --> 00:40:58,944
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll just walk back and forth.

452
00:40:59,564 --> 00:41:00,585
[SPEAKER_01]: What do I do in the meantime?

453
00:41:00,605 --> 00:41:02,126
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I don't know any of these nuances.

454
00:41:03,129 --> 00:41:04,090
[SPEAKER_01]: This is too abstract.

455
00:41:04,810 --> 00:41:06,332
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll watch the soles of my feet.

456
00:41:07,152 --> 00:41:09,054
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll feel the sensations of the ground.

457
00:41:09,394 --> 00:41:11,756
[SPEAKER_01]: I'll feel earthed or connected and stuff.

458
00:41:12,076 --> 00:41:14,979
[SPEAKER_01]: Just anything random I can put into it to give it more meaning.

459
00:41:15,779 --> 00:41:17,921
[SPEAKER_01]: But it was already misconstrued from the start.

460
00:41:18,902 --> 00:41:20,563
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about walking.

461
00:41:20,623 --> 00:41:21,664
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about sitting.

462
00:41:21,704 --> 00:41:23,806
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not even about breathing in ānāpānasati.

463
00:41:23,826 --> 00:41:27,769
[SPEAKER_01]: It's about the implicit peripheral training that you do through your breath.

464
00:41:28,810 --> 00:41:31,152
[SPEAKER_01]: Through the recognition of the body breathing and so on.

465
00:41:36,848 --> 00:41:42,271
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, after the meal, he goes to the forest, to an empty hut, and then he trains his mind...

466
00:41:42,291 --> 00:41:58,019
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is just a condensed way of describing a sustained... of a lifestyle that's sustained away from coarse sensuality, coarse engagement with duties, distractions, irritations, five senses, five sense objects, and so on.

467
00:41:58,399 --> 00:41:59,160
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what it means.

468
00:41:59,180 --> 00:42:05,183
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't mean, literally, I must find the root of a tree where I will plant myself sitting like this or like that, and then do this

469
00:42:05,923 --> 00:42:12,331
[SPEAKER_01]: mechanical method of just adhering to these steps and magical result will occur.

470
00:42:13,413 --> 00:42:17,034
[SPEAKER_01]: No, it just means, oh, this is how you live now.

471
00:42:17,074 --> 00:42:39,618
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a occasional exposure to the right environment, occasional exposure to the... which is good for somebody who is completely immersed in the five cores of sensuality, but it's nowhere near enough to begin to see the animal, the state it is, for what it is accurately, without needing the secondary, as I said, rationalizations and contemplations and reflections.

